ne single. If I play anything but a 2, I create waste, I create a liability. Every card spent to block a 4 gives me a worse hand in the long run. Since the 4 is low, I should pass. My hand is best positioned to fight sets (pairs, triples), or for me to play the 2 when the time is right.\n\nSo, I\'ve made the decision. Pass. Maintain my perfect hand. Let the teammate decide. Preserve my strategy of winning with Full Houses and good pairs. And wait for my moment.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord played a Single 4. Your hand is perfectly structured into two Full Houses (333+44 and 666+77), three pairs (55, KK, AA), and one high single (2). This configuration uses all your cards efficiently without any "waste" singles (low singles that are hard to play). Playing any single card now (like 5, K, or A) would break a pair or triplet, leaving you with an isolated single card and weakening your hand structure. Since the 4 is a low card, your teammate (next player) likely has a spare single to play, or you can wait for a shift to pairs or triplets where your hand is very strong.\n\nReason: To preserve the optimal hand structure of Full Houses and Pairs and avoid creating difficult isolated single cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
